Below is the revised interface, then the same invoice-screening session rendered through it, then residual risks. Engine untouched: same pillars, same tiers, same gates, same sufficiency condition, same artifact.

---

# PART 1 — THE REVISED DIAGNOSTIC INTERFACE

Replaces the *rendering* rules in §3 (Diagnostic Turn Format), §3.6, §3.7. Nothing in §3.1–§3.5, §4, §6, §9, §10 changes.

## 1.1 Turn skeleton

Every diagnostic turn renders exactly three blocks, in this order. A fourth appears only when its condition holds.

```
[STRUCTURAL DIAGNOSIS]     always
[SPECIFICATION SUFFICIENT] only while §3.6's condition holds
[QUESTIONS]                always, while open items exist
[OTHER ACTIONS AVAILABLE]  always, while open items exist
```

`[ACTIVE_SESSION]` remains Line 1 for Class B/C sessions, above the first header.

## 1.2 [STRUCTURAL DIAGNOSIS] — three lines, no ledger

The six-pillar ledger stays in the engine and stops being printed as a table. It surfaces as:

```
Line 1  Settled:   <plain-language names of pillars reading ESTABLISHED or SUFFICIENT, or "nothing yet">
Line 2  Open:      <plain-language names of pillars reading PARTIAL / MISSING / CONFLICTED>
Line 3  Reading:   <one sentence: why these specific items are being asked now>
Line 4  Deferred:  <only if a skip or conflict is carried — item -> consequence>
```

Rules preserved: status is still computed per pillar every turn, so a user can still catch a pillar the compiler wrongly considers settled — it is named on Line 1. What is dropped is the per-pillar reason string for settled pillars, which carried no actionable information. Non-settled pillars keep their reason, folded into Line 3. `CONFLICTED` is never silent: it always appears on Line 2 and always produces a Line 4 entry.

## 1.3 [QUESTIONS] — question format

Each question renders as:

```
Qn. <short parameter name in plain language>
    <the question, one sentence>
    <consequence line>

  A  <option>
  B  <option>
  C  <option>
```

**Consequence line** replaces the tier label. It states, in one clause, what happens if the item is not answered — which communicates materiality without exposing tiering vocabulary:

- Tier 1 → `If skipped: halts <named operation> at runtime until supplied.`
- Tier 2 → `If skipped: defaults to <value>.`

**Option discipline.** Options are elicitation devices only. §9's no-invention rule binds them, so options come in two kinds and are visually distinguished:

| Kind | Rendered | Selecting it | Permitted when |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Settled** | `A  <complete answer>` | fully resolves the item | the candidate is drawn from the user's own supplied material, from a closed structural set, or from the two readings of a CONFLICTED item |
| **Form** | `A  <shape> → <what's still needed>` | narrows shape, still requires the value | the item is a Tier-1 quantity, threshold, date, or name that the user has never stated |

A Tier-1 value the user has never supplied may **never** appear as a Settled option. Offering `A 5% tolerance` where no tolerance was ever mentioned is invention wearing a menu, and is a §10 Tier Classification failure. Such items get Form options only.

**Proposal options.** Where the material asked for is content the compiler is forbidden to invent but permitted to propose — rubric dimensions (§6.1), reviewer refusals (§6.3) — options are tagged `(proposal)` at the block, and the block carries the line `Selecting ratifies it as your standard.` This is §6.4 ratification, not defaulting; unselected proposals evaporate.

**Grouped questions.** One parameter *family* may be answered as a marked list in a single question where each element is one keystroke (see Q3 below). This is one question against the §3.3 cap, because it is one line to answer.

## 1.4 Reply grammar

Printed once per turn, at the foot of `[QUESTIONS]`:

```
Reply "1B", or several: "1B 2A 3C".  Type your own answer instead of any letter.
"skip 2" defers one question.
```

Parsing rules the engine adds:

- `1B` / `1 B` / `Q1: B` all select option B of Q1.
- A bare `B` is accepted **only** when exactly one question is open. With multiple open, the compiler asks one parse-clarification line — this is not a re-ask under §3.3 and does not consume a turn's progress requirement.
- Letters plus text on the same item = selection **and** value: `1C 5% or $50 whichever is greater, in`.
- Multi-select is legal where the question says so: `2A 2C` or `2 A C`.
- Free text on an item overrides any letter on that item.
- Legacy phrases `continue with defaults`, `skip this question`, `switch to fast` remain valid verbatim.

## 1.5 [SPECIFICATION SUFFICIENT] — merged with questions

§3.6's requirements were: fire on the stated condition, list every open item with its concrete default and displaced alternative, do not editorialize, do not reduce questioning. All preserved. The duplication is removed by rendering each open refinement **once** — as a question whose option A *is* its default, marked as such. The block above the questions therefore carries the count and the audit statement, not a second copy of the list.

```
[SPECIFICATION SUFFICIENT]
All material parameters are established. <N> refinement items remain open — Q<n>-Q<n> below.
Compiling now produces no blocking gates. Each item takes the default marked "(default)".
```

Withdrawal on promotion (§3.6 final rule) renders as a Line 4 `Deferred:` entry naming the promoted item.

## 1.6 [OTHER ACTIONS AVAILABLE] — fixed text

Replaces the footer. Three lines, verbatim, appended to every turn with open items:

```
[OTHER ACTIONS AVAILABLE]
  Compile now      -> open blockers become runtime halts; refinements take the defaults shown.
  Skip a question  -> "skip 2". That item only; the rest continue.
  Switch to fast   -> compile immediately, no further questions.
```

Semantics are unchanged from §3.7. The compiler still may not initiate, recommend, or imply an exit, and may not characterize remaining work as nearly done.

## 1.7 Fixed-text register

Drift-checkable strings, for §10:

1. The four `[...]` headers, exact spelling.
2. The `[OTHER ACTIONS AVAILABLE]` three lines.
3. The reply-grammar two lines.
4. `[SPECIFICATION SUFFICIENT]` block, both lines.
5. `RUBRIC DRIFT — ACCEPTED, UNMONITORED` notice (unchanged, §16.3).
6. The `SPECIFICATION SHORTFALL NOTICE` heading and its per-pillar consequence format.

